    <h1>Hrafnsmál: The Norse Raven Poem of King Haraldr Fairhair</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Hrafnsmál</em> ("The Sayings of the Raven"), also called <em>Haraldskvæði</em>, is an Old Norse skaldic poem attributed to Þorbjörn Hornklofi (c. 872–900 CE). It commemorates the Battle of Hafrsfjord using a Valkyrie-raven dialogue: a raven, bloody from the battlefield, reports on King Haraldr Fairhair's victory and his fearsome warriors. It is one of the oldest surviving skaldic poems and a key document of the raven's role in Norse warrior culture.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Poem and Its Context</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Battle of Hafrsfjord (fought near Stavanger, Norway, c. 872 CE) was the decisive battle in which Haraldr Fairhair defeated a coalition of petty kings and chieftains to establish his rule over much of Norway. <em>Hrafnsmál</em> is a court poem celebrating this victory — a poem of praise (<em>drápa</em> or <em>flokkr</em>) in the skaldic tradition, where poets composed intricate verse in honour of kings and their deeds.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The poem survives in fragmentary form, preserved as quotations in later prose works — primarily Snorri Sturluson's <em>Heimskringla</em> (c. 1230 CE) and the <em>Fagrskinna</em> (c. 1220–1230 CE). These later works quote the poem as an authority for details about Haraldr's reign. The original complete poem, if it existed as a single composition, is lost.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven-Narrator Device</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The distinctive feature of <em>Hrafnsmál</em> is its frame narrative: a Valkyrie encounters a raven and questions him. The raven — described as dark-winged, with a blood-stained beak — has just come from the battlefield and reports on what it witnessed. This device allows the skald to use the raven as a dramatic eyewitness, its presence on the field confirming the slaughter of Haraldr's enemies.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The raven-informant figure connects several threads of Norse symbolic thought:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ravens as Odin's messengers (Huginn and Muninn) who fly out and return with news of the world — the battlefield raven reports back, as Odin's ravens report to Odin.</li>
      <li>Ravens as battlefield scavengers who eat the slain — the raven's presence and its bloody beak are evidence of the battle's outcome.</li>
      <li>The Valkyrie's role: she chooses the slain and brings them to Valhöll — her conversation with the raven connects the battlefield dead to Odin's hall.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Berserkers</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Hrafnsmál</em> contains one of the most important early descriptions of the Norse <em>berserkr</em> (berserker warriors). The raven narrator describes Haraldr's warriors — his <em>úlfheðnar</em> (wolf-warriors) and berserkers — as frenzied fighters who bite their shields and are immune to iron and fire in battle. This passage is a primary source for the berserker phenomenon and shows the intimate connection between the raven (Odin's bird) and Odin's warrior-cult.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>The berserker description in <em>Hrafnsmál</em> (reported through the raven's testimony) rhetorically ties Haraldr's military power to divine sanction — his warriors are Odin's own, observed and reported by Odin's birds.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Significance in Raven Tradition</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Hrafnsmál</em> is significant for understanding the Norse raven as a literary and symbolic figure because:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>It shows the raven used as a narrative device in one of the oldest extant skaldic poems — demonstrating the raven's established symbolic role by the late 9th century.</li>
      <li>It explicitly links the battlefield raven with the intelligence-gathering ravens of myth (Huginn and Muninn) through the raven-as-informant device.</li>
      <li>It places the raven in direct dialogue with a Valkyrie — integrating the raven into the Norse afterlife cosmology.</li>
      <li>The poem's title, <em>Hrafnsmál</em>, means "Raven's Speech" or "Raven's Words" — an extraordinary naming choice, placing the raven at the centre of a poem ostensibly about a king's military triumph.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

